742 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 346 0 R
/E (Camouflage image datasets \(COD10K and CAMO\) are downsampled to 128x128 pixels and normalized to a [0,1] range for computational efficiency on edge hardware. Advanced augmentation, including Gaussian blur and random contrast jitter, is applied to simulate field conditions. Each static image is transformed into a temporal spike train over $T=16$ timesteps using Poisson Rate Coding, where pixel intensities determine the firing probability of input spikes, ensuring the network captures subtle texture variations.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
743 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 346 0 R
/Pg 45 0 R
/A [1528 0 R 0]
/K [3]
>>
endobj
744 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (Proposed Methodology)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
745 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (Model Architecture)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
746 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (The system utilizes a 3-layer Spiking Convolutional Neural Network \(SCNN\) architecture optimized for the snnTorch framework. The model employs Leaky Integrate-and-Fire \(LIF\) neurons with learnable membrane decay constants beta to adapt to complex camouflage backgrounds. Training is conducted using Backpropagation Through Time \(BPTT\) with an ArcTan \(ATan\) surrogate gradient function, allowing the network to learn weights effectively across discrete spike events. To maximize energy efficiency, the network is designed for 85-90% temporal sparsity, ensuring that power is only consumed when significant visual features trigger a spiking event, allowing for ultra-low power real-time inference on the Jetson Nano.)
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
747 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 347 0 R
/Pg 46 0 R
/A [1529 0 R 0]
/K [3]
>>
endobj
